  "raw_meta_yaml": "# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prefix-dev/recipe-format/main/schema.json\n\ncontext:\n  name: nccl4py\n  version: \"0.2.0\"\n\npackage:\n  name: ${{ name }}\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  # NVIDIA tags nccl4py releases separately as `nccl4py-vX.Y.Z` on the\n  # NVIDIA/nccl repository; the tarball extracts to `nccl-nccl4py-vX.Y.Z/`\n  # with the Python package at `nccl4py/` in that root.\n  url: https://github.com/NVIDIA/nccl/archive/refs/tags/nccl4py-v${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 190f099968bc6962ca401f0b0f8dd01dbc8f93d2ed8889380f69f98470813c8b\n\nbuild:\n  number: 1\n  skip:\n    - not linux\n    - cuda_compiler_version == \"None\"\n    # - match(python, \"<3.10\")\n    # Build exactly one CUDA variant (13.*). Combined with the\n    # ignore_run_exports below, the resulting package serves both CUDA 12 and\n    # CUDA 13 runtimes, so there's no value in duplicating the build.\n    - not match(cuda_compiler_version, \"13.*\")\n\nrequirements:\n  # nccl4py is CUDA-major-agnostic: its compiled extensions use only opaque\n  # CUDA handle types (cudaStream_t, etc. from driver_types.h) and do not\n  # link libcudart. NCCL is dlopen'd at runtime via cuda-pathfinder, so a\n  # single build can serve either a CUDA 12 or CUDA 13 environment. Drop the\n  # normal run_exports that would otherwise pin cuda-version to the build's\n  # CUDA major (analogous to CUDA Enhanced Compatibility).\n  ignore_run_exports:\n    by_name:\n      - cuda-version\n    from_package:\n      - cuda-cudart-dev\n  build:\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('cxx') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('cuda') }}\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - if: aarch64\n      then:\n        - arm-variant * ${{ arm_variant_type }}\n    - if: build_platform != target_platform\n      then:\n        - cross-python_${{ target_platform }}\n        - cython >=0.29.24\n        - python\n  host:\n    - python\n    - pip\n    - setuptools >=77.0.3\n    - cython >=0.29.24\n    - cuda-version ${{ cuda_compiler_version }}.*\n    - cuda-cudart-dev\n  run:\n    - python\n    - numpy\n    - packaging\n    - cuda-core >=0.5.1,<1\n    - cuda-pathfinder >=1.1,<2\n    - cuda-bindings\n    - cuda-version >=12,<14.0a0\n    # nccl_linux.pyx was code-generated against NCCL 2.30.0 (see header\n    # comment) and dlsym's symbols from that release, including the window\n    # APIs introduced in 2.28 (ncclCommWindowRegister/Deregister). Older\n    # NCCL would load but fail at first call to any post-2.27 symbol.\n    - nccl >=2.30,<3\n\ntests:\n  # Default-env sanity: imports + pip_check. This exercises whichever\n  # cuda-version the solver picks (will be 13.x given the build env).\n  - python:\n      imports:\n        - nccl\n        - nccl.bindings\n        - nccl.core\n      pip_check: true\n  # Verify the single CUDA-13 build resolves cleanly in a CUDA 12 runtime.\n  # This is the payoff of the `ignore_run_exports` above: one artifact is\n  # expected to serve both CUDA majors. The `cuda-version 12.*` constraint\n  # cascades through `nccl` and `cuda-bindings`' own cuda-version deps,\n  # forcing the test env to pick their CUDA-12 variants.\n  #\n  # Using script: instead of python: because rattler-build currently drops\n  # per-test `requirements:` overrides on python: tests — script: tests\n  # honor them, which is what we need here.\n  - script:\n      - python -c \"import nccl, nccl.bindings, nccl.core; print(nccl.__version__)\"\n    requirements:\n      run:\n        - python\n        - cuda-version 12.*\n  # Same, but forcing a CUDA 13 runtime.\n  - script:\n      - python -c \"import nccl, nccl.bindings, nccl.core; print(nccl.__version__)\"\n    requirements:\n      run:\n        - python\n        - cuda-version 13.*\n\nabout:\n  license: BSD-3-Clause\n  license_file: LICENSE.txt\n  summary: Python bindings for NVIDIA NCCL\n  description: |\n    NCCL4Py provides Pythonic access to NCCL for efficient multi-GPU and\n    multi-node collective communication and point-to-point transfers. It\n    ships low-level Cython bindings plus a high-level `nccl.core` API.\n  homepage: https://developer.nvidia.com/nccl\n  documentation: https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/index.html\n  repository: https://github.com/NVIDIA/nccl\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - conda-forge/cuda\n",
